# LINE ENDINGS ARE PINNED, ON EVERY PLATFORM.
#
# Not because anything was broken without it. This was added after a wrong
# diagnosis (FK-16): a Linux process inspecting a Windows clone over a file
# bridge reported 56 files modified, because that process never saw the global
# core.autocrlf=true the Windows machine reads. On the machine that owns the
# files, the tree was clean.
#
# Pinning is still right, for one reason worth stating and no others: every
# platform then checks out IDENTICAL BYTES, so a reading taken from one machine
# describes the other. An unpinned repository quietly makes cross-platform
# inspection unreliable, and this kit is inspected across platforms constantly.
#
# The cost, since it is real, and the first version of this comment got it
# wrong in the direction that matters (FK-31):
#
# A clone that already holds CRLF does NOT go dirty. These attributes apply at
# CHECKOUT and do not rewrite files already on disk, and git trusts its stat
# cache, so those files are never re-read and `git status` stays clean while the
# bytes stay CRLF -- indefinitely, for any file no later commit happens to
# touch. Fourteen of them sat like that on the operator's machine.
#
# This comment used to say the fix was `git reset --hard`. It is not, and that
# sentence was one keystroke from repeating FK-16, which is the finding about
# my telling him to discard his working tree over a line-ending diagnosis I had
# not verified. The repair is per file and non-destructive:
#
#   git ls-files --eol            # i/lf w/crlf is the fault
#   <delete the file, then git checkout -- it>
#
# `git add --renormalize .` does NOT do it: it fixes the index, leaves the
# working tree CRLF, and makes git report clean. Reproduced, not reasoned.
#
# This kit is Python, Markdown and JSON. It has no file that wants CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mov  binary
*.zip  binary
*.pdf  binary
